dict(title="The Boat of the Night", scene="nile", paras=[
"The Egyptians told a beautiful story about the night. They believed that the sun was carried across the sky each day in a boat, sailing over the heavens from east to west. And each evening, when the sun set behind the western cliffs, the boat did not stop. It sailed on, into the night.",
"The story goes that during the hours of darkness, the sun's boat travelled through a hidden world beneath the earth, called the Duat. The night was divided into twelve hours, and each hour was like a different region of that hidden world, with its own gates and guardians, its own rivers and fields. The boat passed through them one by one, slowly, steadily, all through the night.",
"And at the end of the twelfth hour, the boat reached the eastern horizon, and the sun rose again, renewed and shining, to begin a new day. Every sunrise, in this story, was a small rebirth. The night was not an ending, but a journey, and the morning always came.",
"The Egyptians also used the stars to tell the time at night. They watched for particular groups of stars rising, one after another, through the dark hours. Priests whose job was to keep the temple rituals on time would note which stars had appeared, and so they would know how much of the night had passed.",
"There is something very comforting in their idea of the night. That while we sleep, the sun is not gone, only travelling. That the darkness is a passage, carefully guided, hour by hour, toward the light. That somewhere beneath the quiet earth, a little boat is sailing on, and all is well.",
]),

